@{
    ViewBag.Title = "ASP.Net MVC";
}

@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}
<div class="help">
    <span class="mdi-action-info icon-help" title="More information">
        <ul class="info">
            <li><span class="text-danger">***</span>: Guidelines that you should never skip and should be applicable to all situations.</li>
            <li><span class="text-danger">**</span>: Strongly recommended guidelines.</li>
            <li><span class="text-danger">*</span>: Recommended guidelines that may not be applicable in all situations.</li>
        </ul>
    </span>
    <span class="mdi-action-tab-unselected icon-sidenv-right" id="toggle-sidenav-right"></span>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ol class="rules">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title" id="model-recommendation">Model Recommendations</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5000 PUT models into a distinct project <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>For application with a large complex model, it's a good idea to create a separate assembly for the model to avoid accidentally mixing concerns. You can then reference the model assembly in your ASP.NET MVC project.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5001 PUT all validation logic into your model <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>All input validation should occur in the model layer. This includes client side validation, which is essential 
                    to performance. However, client side validation can be not enough so we need to validate on server side.</p>
                    <p>The namespace System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations should be the preferred method for validation. 
                    These annotations are added as attributes to the properties of a model class, as the following example shows:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                        public class Function
                        {
                            [Required]
                            [StringLength(50, MinimumLength = 2,
                            ErrorMessageResourceName = "InputLengthValidationMsg",
                            ErrorMessageResourceType = typeof(ViewResources))]
                            public string FunctionName { get; set; }
                        }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5002 PUT all business login into your model <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>We put our application and business logic into separate layers, a domain layer for business logic 
                    and a service layer for application logic. This abstracts them out from the MVC project completely. We 
                    have so many benefits to do so:</p>
                    <p>The business logic isn't tied to a pattern that could change.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Less duplicated business logic.</li>
                        <li>The View is easier to read when there is no business logic present.</li>
                        <li>Testing business roles is isolated to the model.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>For example, if we want user name cannot be null and we could put the logic in the View as follows:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: html">
                        <div class=”label”>string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.Username) ? string.Empty : Model.Username</div> 
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>So, if another view needs the same business logic like the above, we have to do this again. Or we just need
                     to add some codes in its model:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public class User
                            {
                                private string _username;
                                public string Username
                                {
                                    get
                                    {
                                        return string.IsNullOrEmpty(_username) ? string.Empty : _username;
                                    }
                                    set
                                    {
                                        _username = value;
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>This would greatly simplify the view as shown:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: html">
                        <div class=”label”>Model.Username</div> 
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5003 Define interfaces for data access <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>It is preferred that interfaces be used to expose methods on a provider for data access.</p>
                    <p>Beside, interfaces make your codes be testable.</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public interface IfunctionDataAccess
                            {
                                IEnumerable<function> GetFunctionList(ICriteria criteria);
                                ICommandResult Insert(InsertFunctionCriteria criteria, ICriteria logCriteria);
                                ICommandResult Update(ICriteria criteria, ICriteria logCriteria);
                                ICommandResult Delete(ICriteria criteria, ICriteria logCriteria);
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5004 PUT all session logic in the model <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>As a starting point, here are few of possibilities of session state storage:</p>
                    <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Technique</th>
                                <th>Strengths</th>
                                <th>Weaknesses</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>In Process</td>
                                <td>No additional setup required.</td>
                                <td>Does not work if the web site needs to scale.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Session State Service</td>
                                <td>
                                    <p>Lightweight service runs on each machine in a web farm.</p>
                                    <p>Faster than database session storage.</p>
                                </td>
                                <td>Session data is lost if service goes down.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Database</td>
                                <td>Session data is persisted</td>
                                <td>Slower than session state. Management cost is relatively high.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>And here is the following example:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            Our reusable, serializable object:
                            [Serializable]
                            public class UserProfileSessionData
                            {
                                public int UserId { get; set; }
                                public string EmailAddress { get; set; }
                            }
                            Use case:
                            public class LoginController : Controller
                            {
                                [HttpPost]
                                public ActionResult Login(LoginModel model)
                                {
                                    if (ModelState.IsValid)
                                    {
                                        var profileData = new UserProfileSessionData
                                        {
                                            UserId = model.UserId,
                                            EmailAddress = model.EmailAddress
                                        };
                                        Session["UserProfile"] = profileData;
                                    }
                                }
                                public ActionResult LoggedInStateMessage()
                                {
                                    var profileData = this.Session["UserProfile"] as UserProfileSessionData;
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5005 DECORATE Action Methods with Proper AcceptVerbs Attribute <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>
                        ASP.NET MVC is much more vulnerable comparing to Web Forms. We need to make sure the action method that
                         modifies the data only accepts HttpVerbs.Post and HttpVerbs.Get for reading operations. If security is 
                        too much concern, use the VadilationAntiForgeryToken or Captcha.
                    </p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [HttpPost]
                            public ActionResult AddNewGroup(InsertGroupCriteria criteria)
                            {
                                ICommandResut result = _groupService.Insert(criteria);
                                if (result != null && result.IsSuccessful)
                                {
                                    ViewBag.Success = true;
                                }
                                return View(criteria);
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5006 DECORATE the most frequent Action Methods with OutputCache Attribute <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>
                        Use OutputCache attribute when we are returning the less frequent updated data, prime candidate may be 
                        our home page, feed...etc. We can use it for both Html and Json data types. When using it, only specify 
                        the Cache Profile name, do not specify any other thing, use the web.config output cache session to fine tune it. For example:
                    </p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [AcceptVerbs(HttpVerbs.Get), OutputCache(CacheProfile = "About")]
                            public ActionResult About()
                            {
                                return View();
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>And in web.config:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: xml">
                            <system.web>
                                <caching>
                                    <outputcachesettings>
                                        <outputcacheprofiles>
                                            <clear />
                                            <!-- 15 Seconds -->
                                            <add name="Dashboard"
                                                duration="15"
                                                varybyparam="*"
                                                location="Client"
                                            />
                                        </outputcacheprofiles>
                                    </outputcachesettings>
                                </caching>
                            </system.web>
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5007 DECORATE either the Action or the Controller with Authorize attribute <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>When you mark an action method with AuthorizeAttribute, access to that action method is restricted 
                    to users who are both authenticated and authorized. </p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [NapSuperAuth]
                            public class FunctionController : BaseController
                            {
                            }
                            [NapAuth(FunctionKey = FunctionKeys.ManageFunction, Policy = Constant.Policy.Red)]
                            public ActionResult Manage(GetBySiteFunctionCriteria criteria)
                            {
                                return View(criteria);
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        If you mark a controller with the attribute, all action methods in the controller are restricted. 
                        Within a controller that is marked with the AuthorizeAttribute attribute, you can use the 
                        AllowAnonymousAttribute attribute to specify that a particular action method is not restricted to only authorize users.
                    </p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [Authorize]
                            public class FunctionController : BaseController
                            {
                                public ActionResult Manage(GetBySiteFunctionCriteria criteria)
                                {
                                    return View(criteria);
                                }
                                [AllowAnonymous]
                                public ActionResult Update(UpdateFunctionCriteria criteria)
                                {
                                    return View();
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">View Recommendations</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5008 PUT HTML in Views and Partial Views (not in a controller) <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>The strength of view pattern is the readability of view template files and the partial view
                     is a powerful extensibility and reuse mechanism. For example, we can include the same view in an 
                    admin view, without writing another line of code.</p>
                    <p>The following example shows us how to call a partial view:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            &#64;{   Html.RenderPartial("~/Views/Report/CurrencyWinLoss/Main.Header.cshtml", Model); }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5009 ENABLE (auto-generated) client side validation <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>Previously, web developers were faced with the dilemma of keeping client and server validation synchronized. 
                    Starting with ASP.NET MVC 2, it has become easy to add client side validation. Here is the example:</p>
                    <p>First, we need to enable client side validation in web.config:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: xml">
                            <appsettings>
                                <add key="ClientValidationEnabled" value="true" />
                                <add key="UnobtrusiveJavaScriptEnabled" value="true" />
                            </appsettings>
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>And here is out Login form:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            &#64;using (Html.BeginForm())
                            {
                                <div class="form-row">
                                    &#64;Html.LabelFor(m => m.UserName)
                                    &#64;Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.UserName, new { &#64;class = "inputtext" })
                                    &#64;Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.UserName, "<< " + ViewResources.required, new { id = "valMsgUserName" })
                                </div>
                                Html.ValidationSummary(true, ViewResources.LoginUnsuccessMsg, new { id = "msgBox" });
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Now if we try to edit a form's field which does not match the data annotation, the client side 
                    validation will fail and immediate feedback is given to the user.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5010 INSERT server-side comments in templates <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>User server side comments in the view templates for commenting. These comments are stripped out 
                    before the server returns the HTML representation.</p>
                    <p>The following line demonstrates a server side comment:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: html">
                            <%-- This is a server side template comment --%> 
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Do not use HTML comments in the view template because they will be rendered to the web browser 
                    and could be viewed by an advanced (and potentially malicious) user.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5011 USE HTMLHelper extension methods <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>The System.Web.Mvc.Html class contains useful HTML extension methods. These extension methods include helpers for:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Form generation (BeginForm).</li>
                        <li>Input filed generation (chechbox, hidden, radio button, textbox).</li>
                        <li>Link generation (ActionLink).</li>
                        <li>XSS Protection (Encode).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>These HTML Helpers should be utilized as much as possible. For example, the following code creates a link 
                    using the route table back to the default action on the controller from which the view is called. 
                    Here is the example:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            &#64;using (Html.BeginForm())
                            {
                                Html.Hidden("Hidden", "Hidden");
                                Html.ActionLink("Home", "Index", "Home");
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div> 
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">Controller Recommendations</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5012 USE model binding instead of manually parsing the request <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>
                        ASP.NET MVC abstracts much of the rote code of object deserialization by using model binding.
                        Model binding is a mechanism by which request context data is marshaled through reflection into the
                        object type defined on the action method.
                    </p>
                    <p>Here is the following example: We have User class.</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public class User
                            {
                                private string _username;
                                public string Username
                                {
                                    get
                                    {
                                        return string.IsNullOrEmpty(_username) ? string.Empty : _username;
                                    }
                                    set
                                    {
                                        _username = value;
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>And a view model class:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public class ManageUserViewModel
                            {
                                private List<user> _userList;
                                public ManageUserViewModel()
                                {
                                    _userList = new List<user>();
                                }
                                public List<user> UserList
                                {
                                    get
                                    {
                                        return _userList;
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Then we have an action method which calling above view model:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public ActionResult ManageUser()
                            {
                                var userList = _userService.GetAllUsers();
                                var model = new ManageUserViewModel();
                                model.UserList.AddRange(userList);
                                return View(model);
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        The model binding system also runs the validation logic that has been applied to the model object,
                        such as the data annotations attributes. It also has a rich extensibility mechanism that allows full
                        customization of how object are created, populated and validated.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5013 USE Post/Redirect/Get (PRG) when submitting forms (exclude case using ajax) <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>Here are the examples:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>HTTP GET of “/products/create”. “Create” view is rendered.</li>
                        <li>HTTP POST to “/products/submit”.</li>
                        <li>Validation Fails, redirect to “products/create”. “Create” view is rendered.</li>
                        <li>HTTP POST to “/products/submit”.</li>
                        <li>Item is create, redirect to “/products/confirm”.”Confirm” view is rendered.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5014 IMPLEMENT HandleUnknownAction and HandleError <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>The default response for the unknown action is 404 (Not Found) error. If we override the HandleUnknownAction 
                    class in a Controller, we can implement a “default” view in this error. Additionally, put the HandleError 
                    attribute on an action and/ or Controller and we can provide a standard error view for when a uncaught 
                    exception is thrown. Then, here is the example:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public class BaseController : Controller
                            {
                                protected override void HandleUnknownAction(string actionName)
                                {
                                    var strError = string.Format("Unable to find action {0}", actionName);
                                    Logger.Log(strError);
                                    throw new ObjectNotFoundException(strError);
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5015 KEEP controllers free from HttpContext and its tail <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>If you need to access any information from HttpContext, you can use custom filter or create some 
                    interfaces and wrappers to pass in your controller. You can see it in the following example:</p>
                    <p>We have a controller method is declared as:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [AcceptVerbs(HttpVerbs.Get), OutputCache(CacheProfile = "Dashboard"), UserNameFilter]
                            public ActionResult Dashboard(string userName, StoryListTab tab, OrderBy orderBy, int? page)
                            {
                                return View();
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>The UsernameFilter is responsible for passing the Username:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public class UsernameFilter : ActionFilterAttribute
                            {
                                public override void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext filterContext)
                                {
                                    const string Key = "userName";
                                    if (filterContext.ActionParameters.ContainsKey(Key))
                                    {
                                        if (filterContext.HttpContext.User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
                                        {
                                            filterContext.ActionParameters[Key] = filterContext.HttpContext.User.Identity.Name;
                                        }
                                    }
                                    base.OnActionExecuting(filterContext);
                                }
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5016 KEEP your controller to be a single responsibility <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>All methods you can put in your controller is for validating input, calling model while preparing view 
                    and return view or redirect to another action.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">Routing Recommendations</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5017 ORDER routes from specific to general when using standard routing <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public static void RegisterRoutes(RouteCollection routes)
                            {
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    null,
                                    "",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List", category = (string)null, page = 1 }
                                    );
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    null,
                                    "Page{page}",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List", category = (string)null },
                                    new { page = &#64;"\d+" }
                                    );
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    null,
                                    "{category}",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List", page = 1 }
                                    );
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    null,
                                    "{category}/Page{page}",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List" },
                                    new { page = &#64;"\d+" }
                                    );
                            }
                         </pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>The general route should be on top and specific route should be place in bottom.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5018 USE named route mechanism to avoid route ambiguity <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>For example, the following route mapping defines named routes:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            public static void RegisterRoutes(RouteCollection routes)
                            {
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    "Default",
                                    "",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List", category = (string)null, page = 1 }
                                    );
                                routes.MapRoute(
                                    "PageRoute",
                                    "Page{page}",
                                    new { controller = "Products", action = "List", category = (string)null },
                                    new { page = &#64;"\d+" }
                                    );
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
            </ul>            
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">Extensibility Recommendations</h2>            
            <p>OW5019 USE filter for adding behaviors <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
            <p>Filters can be applied to a specific action method to alter its behavior. Alternatively, a filter can 
            be applied to a controller class, in which case it will take effect on every action method in the 
            controller class. Base classes can be used to define common behavior patterns by applying filters 
            to the base controller class and then ensuring that other controllers derive from the base class.</p>
            <p>For example, suppose that you want to add functionality to log information for each request to debug 
            a problem with HTTP headers. The following codes define class that derives from the ActionFilterAttribute class:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public class LogHeadersFilterAttribute : ActionFilterAttribute
                    {
                        public override void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext filterContext)
                        {
                            foreach (string header in
                            filterContext.HttpContext.Request.Headers.AllKeys)
                            {
                                Debug.WriteLine("Header " + header);
                                Debug.WriteLine("Value " +
                                        filterContext.HttpContext.Request.Headers.Get(header));
                            }
                            base.OnActionExecuting(filterContext);
                        }
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">Extensibility Recommendations</h2>
            <p>OW5020 USE ASP.NET special resource folders and resource files <span class="text-danger">*</span></p>
            <img src="~/content/customizedcontrols/images/resource.jpg">
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title">Performance Recommendations</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5021 CONSIDER partial page updates using AJAX for reducing bandwidth <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>One way to mitigate performance issues involving both server processing and request size is to use 
                    asynchronous JavaScript (AJAX) to do partial page updates. ASP.NET MVC has built-in AJAX support, which 
                    help the facilities. The performance benefit occurs because the pattern reduces the amount of processing 
                    that the server must do to render a request and reduce the size of the HTML fragment.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5022 DO NOT overuses session, instead use TempData for short live (intra-request) storage <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>The problem with placing these objects in the Session is that can bog down the server by having to store 
                    extra information that may only be necessary across a redirect. The correct way to store these temporary 
                    variables across a redirect is by using the TempData dictionary.</p>
                    <p>For example:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [HttpPost]
                            public ActionResult LogIn(Seller person)
                            {
                                TempData["name"] = person.Name;
                                return RedirectToAction("ItemUpload");
                            }
                            public ActionResult ItemUpload()
                            {
                                string name = TempData["name"].ToString();
                                ViewBag.Name = name;
                                return View();
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5023 USE an OutputCache Filter for static pages <span class="text-danger">**</span></p>
                    <p>Use OutputCache attribute when you are returning less frequently updated data; your home page 
                    would be a good candidate. This technique can be used for both HTML and JSON data types. You should 
                    only specify the cache profile name while using it; do not specify anything else. You can use the output 
                    cache session of the Web.config file if you need to fine tune caching.</p>
                    <p>For example:</p>
                    <div class="example-code">
                        <pre class="brush: csharp">
                            [AcceptVerbs(HttpVerbs.Get), OutputCache(CacheProfile = "Dashboard")]
                            public ActionResult Dashboard(string userName, StoryListTab tab, OrderBy orderBy, int? page)
                            {
                            }
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>OW5024 CONSIDER using asynchronous controllers for long running requests <span class="text-danger">***</span></p>
                    <p>ASP.NET's threading pool has a default limit of 12 concurrent worker threads per CPU. When the requests 
                    overload the server's ability to process them, a queue is built up of requests.</p>
                    <p>When the queue gets too large (5000 requests pending), the server starts responding with 503 (server too busy) errors.</p>
                    <p>In ASP.NET 4, the number of concurrent threads is set by default to 5000. It is possible to increase the 
                    default limits, there is a better way to mitigate long running requests by using asynchronous.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>
